wq#1655 — [Unit]/[Path] stanza changes for fleet-inventory-render.{service,path}.
Live content captured via `systemctl cat fleet-inventory-render.service
fleet-inventory-render.path` 2026-09-11 (see RUNBOOK.md for the full pre-image).

--- a/etc/systemd/system/fleet-inventory-render.service
+++ b/etc/systemd/system/fleet-inventory-render.service
@@ [Unit]
 Description=Fleet inventory: render machines/*.yaml -> www/fleet.html (wq#1455)
 Documentation=file:///home/lumen/fleet/inventory/DESIGN_SOT.md
 After=local-fs.target
+# wq#1655: a burst of PathChanged triggers (13 writes in ~15s, wq#1580) must
+# never permanently fail this oneshot; the trigger-rate guard lives on the
+# .path unit instead (TriggerLimitIntervalSec/TriggerLimitBurst below).
+StartLimitIntervalSec=0

--- a/etc/systemd/system/fleet-inventory-render.path
+++ b/etc/systemd/system/fleet-inventory-render.path
@@ [Unit]
 Description=Fleet inventory: re-render on inventory change (wq#1455)
+# wq#1655: absorb rapid successive PathChanged events (e.g. a multi-file
+# YAML batch write) without tripping systemd's default per-unit start limit,
+# which is what actually failed 2026-09-10 05:39:45Z (unit-start-limit-hit)
+# and then stayed failed forever, since .path units never self-recover.
+TriggerLimitIntervalSec=10s
+TriggerLimitBurst=20

Notes:
- `StartLimitIntervalSec=0` on the .service disables its own start-rate
  failure mode entirely — a oneshot render is cheap and idempotent, so there
  is no runaway-restart risk worth guarding against on that side.
- `TriggerLimitIntervalSec=`/`TriggerLimitBurst=` (systemd.path(5), systemd
  >= 236) are the .path unit's own trigger-rate guard, separate from
  StartLimit*. 20 triggers per 10s comfortably absorbs the wq#1580 case (13
  writes in ~15s) while still bounding a genuine runaway loop. Exceeding it
  stops the path unit from triggering further (does not itself require a
  reset-failed the way the old default StartLimit did).
- Neither change touches ExecStart, WorkingDirectory, or the PathChanged=
  target list — render.py itself is untouched; this is a systemd-level fix,
  not a render.py debounce (the ticket's "or debounce in render.py"
  alternative was not taken because the systemd-level fix covers the same
  failure with less surface area).
